WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.649
A net-new device should arrive at a known

2
00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:05.563
baseline before anyone
uses it, in two halves.

3
00:00:05.563 --> 00:00:07.512
Compliance decides access.

4
00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:09.491
First set the tenant-wide setting,

5
00:00:09.491 --> 00:00:13.375
mark devices with no compliance
policy assigned as Not compliant,

6
00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:17.189
so an unassigned device can't pass
Conditional Access by default;

7
00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:21.638
Microsoft recommends this whenever
compliance feeds Conditional Access.

8
00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:27.094
The Windows policy typically requires
BitLocker, Secure Boot and code integrity;

9
00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:29.823
firewall, TPM, antivirus and antispyware;

10
00:00:29.823 --> 00:00:34.799
current Defender intelligence with
real-time protection; and a minimum OS.

11
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:38.950
With Defender for Endpoint connected,
add a machine risk score ceiling.

12
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.225
Configuration sets the device up.

13
00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:44.043
Start from the Windows security baseline,

14
00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:48.712
version 25H2 being the latest, and
the Defender for Endpoint baseline.

15
00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:52.929
Existing baseline profiles don't
move to a new version automatically,

16
00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:57.874
and different baselines can set the same
setting differently, so review overlaps.

17
00:00:57.874 --> 00:01:00.628
Then BitLocker with escrow to Entra ID,

18
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:04.500
Windows Hello for Business,
firewall and Windows LAPS.

19
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.963
Because these devices never had Group
Policy, there's nothing to conflict with.

20
00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:10.113
That's the payoff.

21
00:01:10.113 --> 00:01:13.712
Four exception paths cover what
can't follow the main path,

22
00:01:13.712 --> 00:01:18.437
each with written justification, a
compensating control and an audit entry.

23
00:01:18.437 --> 00:01:22.050
A: personal devices,
governed at lower assurance.

24
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.550
B: Linux that Intune
can't manage, under Arc.

25
00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.080
C: Server 2019 and 2022, under
Arc without Entra sign-in,

26
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.375
which is Microsoft's support boundary.

27
00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:39.088
D: Windows that can't enroll, such as
instrument PCs or disconnected enclaves.

28
00:01:39.088 --> 00:01:42.063
Path D can't pass a
compliant-device policy.

29
00:01:42.063 --> 00:01:43.574
So isolate it first.

30
00:01:43.574 --> 00:01:47.235
Manage its Defender settings through
security settings management,

31
00:01:47.235 --> 00:01:48.875
which doesn't make it compliant.

32
00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:53.773
And if its users must reach a protected
resource, record a named exclusion, paired

33
00:01:53.773 --> 00:01:58.580
with a policy that blocks that group from
every device not on the approved list.
